        "title": "Ancient Greek Philosophy - Pre-Socratics, Socrates, Plato, Aristotle",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nPRE-SOCRATICS (600–400 BCE)\nWhat is the world made of?\n  Thales (water) ─── Anaximander (apeiron) ─── Anaximenes (air)\n  Pythagoras (number) ─── Heraclitus (fire / flux / logos)\n  Parmenides (Being is one, unchanging) ─── Zeno (motion is impossible?)\n  Empedocles (four elements) ─── Anaxagoras (mind = nous)\n  Democritus + Leucippus (atoms + void)\n         |\n         v\n  SOCRATES (469–399 BCE)\n  Not: \"what is the world made of?\" but \"how should we live?\"\n  Method: elenchus (cross-examination to expose ignorance)\n  Left no writings. Known through Plato and Xenophon.\n         |\n         v\n  PLATO (428–348 BCE)\n  Pupil of Socrates. Founded the Academy (~388 BCE).\n  Theory of Forms: reality behind appearances.\n         |\n         v\n  ARISTOTLE (384–322 BCE)\n  Pupil of Plato. Founded the Lyceum (~335 BCE).\n  Tutor of Alexander the Great.\n  Rejects the Theory of Forms: universals are in things, not beyond them.\n```"
